/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-OPEN-DECISION-ARM-SET-2026-09-10.md:21:## What the second reviewer adds that costs nothing
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-OPEN-DECISION-ARM-SET-2026-09-10.md:23:- **A matched-anchor tier.** On common anchors the prefix and catalogue are identical across arms, so each catalogue row's realised outcome can be evaluated once and every arm's choice becomes a lookup. **Nine arms then cost about one.** It answers the first and third components cleanly but cannot answer the second, whose value is multi-step by construction.
/home/sat/mcrl-records/decisions/V025-CONTROLLER-OPEN-DECISION-ARM-SET-2026-09-10.md:28:The two are not as far apart as they look. The first answers "what tests the requirement as written"; the second answers "what would the result mean". Both are needed. The cheap path is the matched-anchor tier, which makes the singles nearly free and so removes the cost objection to running them — leaving only the interpretation question, which the degeneracy screen answers.
