/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP-2026-09-10.md:74:An initial control attempt used a 4.9 GB address-space limit and stopped after 7 anchors when NumPy could not reserve a 1.22 MiB array despite RSS being only 1.654 GiB. Address space is not RSS; the partial receipt was resumed after replacing that unsuitable limit with the RSS watchdog. No reported metric came from a failed or partial width.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP-2026-09-10.md:86:The untimed `1.66°` constructions reproduce the standing PANELCEIL aggregates exactly to the stored binary64 values: base, both fixed points, joint-from-first, coordination `+1.944795%`, and traversal gap `+0.905261%`. The fresh actual-10-second control is `13.187489 Mbit/J`, so this run's acceleration ceiling is `+1.840863%`, not the earlier `+1.045609%` obtained when the same search completed more moves within ten wall-clock seconds.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP-2026-09-10.md:92:- **The ceiling question is answered yes.** The conclusion does not depend only on the wall-clock checkpoint: the deterministic coordination ceiling itself reaches `+11.506880%` at `3.32°`.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP-2026-09-10.md:93:- The large acceleration ceilings are partly computational. Wider-beam search paths took much longer on several anchors, while the 10-second construction credits only moves whose evaluations completed by the checkpoint. These are valid measurements on this machine under the required live-load conditions, but their exact percentages should not be assumed hardware- or load-invariant.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP-2026-09-10.md:100:The declared grid, stated before execution and never changed, was `1.66°, 2.40°, 3.32°`. The frozen development panel is `V025_PROBE/world/1`, steps 0–3, crossed in declared order with `nearest-eligible`, `stay-if-possible`, and `random-masked` (12 anchors, 100 users each). No evaluation-only claim date was opened.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-beam-ws/BEAM-WIDTH-CEILING-SWEEP-2026-09-10.md:109:Both start from the same legal base and use exact `F = decoded bits − eta_ref × joules` with the unchanged base-served guard. The 10-second incumbent is the last first-improvement move whose evaluation completed by 10 seconds. The joint selector is reseeded from the certified first fixed point and exhausts the unchanged bounded catalogue: same-beam occupant subsets of size 2–4 with the 1,024-generation cap, victim plus top-2/top-3 physical nominal contributors, complete-beam evacuations, stable assignment order, batches of 32, and the 4,096 unique-candidate cap.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/CEILING-30-DATES-2026-09-10.md:1:# Coordination headroom diagnostic across 30 TRAIN dates: mean +5.888%, 95% CI [+5.235%, +6.540%]
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/CEILING-30-DATES-2026-09-10.md:5:Across 30 dates, the arithmetic mean of the per-date pooled ORACLE_SET-over-UNILATERAL gains is **+5.888%** (date-level sample SD **1.747%**, two-sided 95% Student-t CI **[+5.235%, +6.540%]**). The date is the independent unit: each date first pools bits and joules across its anchors, computes each arm's pooled bit/J, and then forms the ORACLE_SET/UNILATERAL relative gain. This interval is descriptive over the selected dates, not a population-randomization interval.
/home/sat/mcrl-v025-ceiling30-ws/CEILING-30-DATES-2026-09-10.md:9:| Panel | Dates | Mean gain | Date SD | 95% t CI over dates | Minimum | Maximum |
