 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# External primary-source evidence for BEAMCAP
     2	
     3	Completion criterion: this note identifies the strongest primary/official evidence found for Ka-band multibeam LEO per-beam occupancy/capacity mechanisms, gives exact source URLs, and states explicitly whether it yields a defensible commercial simultaneous-users-per-beam limit.
     4	
     5	## Bottom line
     6	
     7	No defensible public primary-source number was found for the maximum number of *simultaneously served user terminals per beam in an operational commercial Ka-band multibeam LEO network*. In particular, none of the sources below supports treating 10, 12.5, 20, or 25 users per beam as a universal commercial hard cap.
     8	
     9	The official material nevertheless makes one narrower and useful conclusion possible: **about 12.5 users per active beam is not, by user count alone, an obviously nonphysical regime.** 3GPP's NTN study uses at least 10 UEs per beam for S/Ka system-level calibration, reports LEO-600 Ka throughput results with 10 UEs per cell under proportional-fair scheduling, and has a Ka-VSAT link-budget calibration with 10 UEs transmitting simultaneously in adjacent frequency resources. Elsewhere the same NTN study reports LEO simulations with 15 and 20 UEs per cell. These are study/calibration assumptions, not fielded-system limits or service guarantees.
    10	
    11	## 1. 3GPP TR 38.821: direct Ka/LEO occupancy evidence
    12	
    13	Primary source: 3GPP, *TR 38.821 V16.2.0 (2023-03), Solutions for NR to support non-terrestrial networks (NTN)*. [Official specification record](https://portal.3gpp.org/desktopmodules/Specifications/SpecificationDetails.aspx?specificationId=3525); [exact official archive package for the cited version](https://www.3gpp.org/ftp/Specs/archive/38_series/38.821/38821-g20.zip). A directly readable official-organizational-partner copy of V16.0.0, containing the same relevant tables, is [ATIS.3GPP.38.821.V1600](https://www.atis.org/wp-content/uploads/3gpp-documents/Rel16/ATIS.3GPP.38.821.V1600.pdf). The locally retained V16.2.0 PDF is `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/ref/official/38821-g20.pdf`; its page numbers and a locally derived Markdown conversion were checked directly.
    14	
    15	- Table 6.1.1.1-5, pp. 37-39, governs both S-band and Ka-band system-level calibration. It sets Ka-band DL/UL maximum bandwidth per beam to 400 MHz (subject to frequency/polarization reuse) and says the UE-distribution baseline is “at least X=10 UEs per beam”; the Ka terminal configuration is VSAT. Local conversion: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/official-markdown/38821-g20/part-000.md:1625-1677`.
    16	- Section 6.1.1.3, Table 6.1.1.3-1, p. 48, says two companies supplied DL user-throughput results; one result set assumed 10 UEs per cell and proportional-fair scheduling. The table explicitly includes LEO-600 Ka-band study cases 6 and 7. At approximately 50% resource utilisation, its per-UE throughput percentiles are 6.91/10.03/13.7 Mbit/s for case 6 and 4.76/7.75/11.22 Mbit/s for case 7 (5th/50th/95th percentiles). Local conversion: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/official-markdown/38821-g20/part-001.md:45-62`.
    17	- Table 6.1.3.2-1, pp. 53-54, defines a Ka-band VSAT link-budget calibration at 20 GHz DL / 30 GHz UL with 400 MHz system bandwidth. For VSAT uplink, each terminal is allocated one tenth of the beam bandwidth; devices within a beam receive adjacent frequency resources; CIR is averaged over **10 simultaneously transmitting UEs** in the reference beam. Local conversion: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/official-markdown/38821-g20/part-001.md:320-370`.
    18	- Section 6.2.3 reports a LEO-1200 system-level study with 15 and 20 UEs per cell under proportional-fair scheduling and no frequency reuse, and specifically discusses results “with 15 UEs per beam.” This passage does not identify that particular study as Ka-band, so it is supporting NTN/LEO evidence rather than a Ka-specific count. Local conversion: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/official-markdown/38821-g20/part-001.md:940-954`.
    19	- Section 7.3.2.1.6 says many devices may be served within a single NTN cell and discusses a worst-case mobility stress calculation with 65,519 connected UEs, reflecting the then-current maximum C-RNTI value. This is a connected-UE control-plane stress assumption, **not** simultaneous data service and not a throughput-capacity claim. Local conversion: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/official-markdown/38821-g20/part-002.md:267-315`.
    20	
    21	Interpretation: 3GPP models occupancy through scheduled radio resources and resource utilisation. Ten simultaneously transmitting Ka VSATs in one beam is explicit. Ten UEs per cell in the LEO-600 Ka throughput study and 15/20 UEs in another LEO study bracket the project's 12.5-users/active-beam point in a broad standards-study sense. They do **not** prove that the project's bandwidth, antenna, ACM, interference, or rate results match a commercial system; those must be assessed separately.
    22	
    23	Numerically, 12.5 is 25% above the explicit 10-UE Ka/LEO calibration occupancy. That modest ratio is useful only to reject “headcount alone makes it impossible”; it is not a 25% capacity-margin claim.
    24	
    25	## 2. Telesat Lightspeed: official description of a real Ka-band LEO resource mechanism
    26	
    27	Primary source: Telesat, *Telesat Lightspeed Service Resiliency* (official operator PDF), <https://www.telesat.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Telesat-Lightspeed-Resiliency.pdf>.
    28	
    29	- The document identifies Lightspeed as a Ka-band LEO system (p. 1).
    30	- It describes resources adapting to user needs and the operating environment, and says terminal burst-time plans and satellite beam-hop plans can be changed in near real time so that lower-performing channels receive more slots (p. 2).
    31	
    32	Interpretation: this first-party system description supports a dynamic scheduled-slot/beam-hopping model of finite capacity. It does not publish a maximum simultaneous terminal count per beam, so it cannot justify a numeric cap.
    33	
    34	A second first-party document, [Telesat Lightspeed Communications Security Features](https://www.telesat.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Communications-Security-Features-Whitepaper-Telesat-Lightspeed.pdf), says each satellite has 24 agile physical user beams that can hop among as many as 576 virtual beams, gives approximately 10 Tbit/s total network capacity, and says suitable terminals can receive more than 1 Gbit/s. It likewise states no users-per-beam cap. These are architectural/marketing design statements, useful for mechanism and scale but not a field measurement.
    35	
    36	## 3. ETSI DVB-RCS2: official satellite return-link slot assignment
    37	
    38	Primary source: ETSI, *EN 301 545-2 V1.4.1, Digital Video Broadcasting (DVB); Second Generation DVB Interactive Satellite System (DVB-RCS2); Part 2: Lower Layers for Satellite standard*, <https://www.etsi.org/deliver/etsi_en/301500_301599/30154502/01.04.01_60/en_30154502v010401p.pdf>.
    39	
    40	- Section 7.2.2.1, p. 117, defines MF-TDMA as RCSTs sharing carriers divided into timeslots, with the network control centre assigning timeslots.
    41	- Section 7.6.5.1, p. 166, states that every active RCST is provided dynamically parameterized timeslot/burst pairs.
    42	
    43	Interpretation: this official satellite-access standard again makes the finite resource a schedule of carrier/time slots, not a universal terminals-per-beam integer. It is not specific to LEO or to the exact forward-link physics in V0.25 and therefore should be cited only for the general capacity mechanism.
    44	
    45	## 4. 3GPP TS 38.214: NR allocates time/frequency resources per scheduled UE
    46	
    47	Primary source: 3GPP, *TS 38.214 V19.3.0, NR; Physical layer procedures for data*. Exact official archive URL for the checked version: <https://www.3gpp.org/ftp/Specs/archive/38_series/38.214/38214-j30.zip>. Local official PDF: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/paper-source/ref/official/38214-j30.pdf`.
    48	
    49	- Clause 5.1.2.2.1 says downlink resource-allocation type 0 carries a bitmap of resource-block groups allocated to the scheduled UE (PDF p. 38 in the extracted text region).
    50	- Clause 5.1.2.2.2 says downlink type 1 indicates to a scheduled UE a contiguous set of virtual resource blocks within the active bandwidth part.
    51	- Clause 6.1.2.2 similarly defines uplink frequency-domain resource-block assignment; clauses 6.1.2.2.1 and 6.1.2.2.2 allocate resource-block groups/blocks to a scheduled UE.
    52	
    53	Interpretation: NR's data plane exposes time/frequency scheduling. The standard does not supply a universal hard maximum UEs per satellite beam. A system may of course have implementation-specific scheduler, control-channel, modem, gateway, power, or QoS limits.
    54	
    55	## 5. Project Kuiper: applicant-authored Ka-LEO system filing
    56	
    57	Primary source: Kuiper Systems LLC, *Technical Appendix to Application for Authority to Launch and Operate a Non-Geostationary Satellite Orbit System in Ka-band Frequencies*, filed with the FCC, <https://fcc.report/IBFS/SAT-LOA-20190704-00057/1773885.pdf?raw=1>. The FCC's authorization confirms the applicant/system and Ka customer bands: [FCC 20-102](https://docs.fcc.gov/public/attachments/FCC-20-102A1.pdf), paragraphs 9-10 (pp. 2-3).
    58	
    59	- The applicant describes Ka-band LEO user beams giving multiple active customers connectivity within a virtual spot (Technical Appendix p. 8).
    60	- It describes variable user-channel bandwidth and assignment in proportion to customer count and demand (p. 10).
    61	- Each downlink user beam can be “gigabit-class” under stated link conditions; 100 MHz channels can be aggregated to 200-500 MHz. Return-link resources use 50 MHz channels aggregable to 200 MHz and can be shared among multiple terminals by FDMA/TDMA and potentially CDMA, adapting from small user populations to larger user bases (pp. 24-25). User-channel allocation is performed within each beam (p. 30).
    62	
    63	Interpretation: this is first-party design evidence from an actual authorized Ka-band LEO system that beams serve multiple active customers and scale by flexible bandwidth/multiple-access scheduling. It publishes no terminals-per-beam maximum. At the project's nominal setpoint, 12.5 users x 50 Mbit/s = 625 Mbit/s aggregate, which is below the filing's conditional “gigabit-class” beam description; that is merely an order-of-magnitude aggregate sanity check, **not** validation of this project's geometry, link budget, ACM, power, overhead, or achieved rate.
    64	
    65	## 6. Predecessor-project locations found incidentally
    66	
    67	These locations are useful to the Part 1 investigator; no checkpoint was opened.
    68	
    69	- The predecessor record explicitly says Cap25 was a project-created stress-test extension to address shared-Q geometric collapse, not a baseline-paper or 3GPP commercial deployment value: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/artifacts/literature_grounding.md:3-22` (especially lines 9, 11, 21-22).
    70	- The operative selector is an opt-in centralized action-assignment constraint, not a PHY carrier count or reward term: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/src/modqn_paper_reproduction/algorithms/modqn.py:308-352`. It sets `max_users_per_beam` from config, ranks valid actions, and chooses the first whose assigned count is below that value. The dispatch is enabled only by `anti_collapse_action_constraint_enabled` plus mode `capacity-aware-greedy-assignment`: same file, lines 501-545.
    71	- The matched protocol records a value of 25 and 100 total users: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/docs/research/angle-aware-ee-multicatfish/07-handover-aware-ee-hobs-sibling-sdd.md:1145-1172`.
    72	- A distinct soft load-balance reward also existed. The environment documents `r3 = -(max_beam_thr - min_beam_thr) / U` and implements it separately from the cap: `/home/sat/modqn-paper-exploration/src/modqn_paper_reproduction/env/step.py:18-22` and `:829-870`.
    73	
    74	Important code nuance: `_select_capacity_constrained_actions` initializes `selected` to the top-ranked action and, if no valid action has remaining count, retains that selection and increments its count. Thus the intended cap is hard whenever the valid-action/capacity set leaves an under-cap choice (as intended in the recorded protocol), but the function itself has no explicit infeasibility rejection when all valid candidates are saturated. It is an action-decoder admission/assignment guard, not a resource scheduler inside the physical environment.
    75	
    76	## Conclusion usable in the main report
    77	
    78	- **Verified from primary/official sources:** Ka-band multibeam NTN standards work explicitly evaluates 10 UEs per beam, including 10 simultaneously transmitting Ka VSATs on adjacent frequency resources; related LEO studies use 15 and 20 UEs per cell. Real Ka-band LEO design material describes dynamic slot/beam-plan allocation.
    79	- **Inference, bounded:** the number 12.5 users per active beam does not by itself make the crowded endpoint physically impossible. Whether that endpoint is meaningful service depends on its per-user rate, resource share, ACM/power feasibility, and interference—not merely its user count.
    80	- **Not found:** a defensible official public maximum simultaneous-users-per-beam figure for a deployed commercial Ka-band multibeam LEO network. Do not present 10, 15, 20, or the predecessor's 25 as such a maximum.
    81	
    82	Criterion check: satisfied—primary/official evidence, exact URLs, and the explicit no-commercial-count finding are present.

